    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d282">

                <pb facs="0468" n="374" sameAs="frus:frus1924v02/pg374"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.945/1045: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Woods</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-04-15T13:00:00+09:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 15,
                                1924—1 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received April 15—6:54
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>69. My 68, April 14, 2 p.m.<anchor n="43" xml:id="d282-f1"/> Although Japanese
                    public in general and newspapers in particular apparently expected the House of
                    Representatives to pass the Johnson bill,<anchor n="44" xml:id="d282-f2"/> and
                    yesterday the press comments in that connection were surprisingly moderate, this
                    morning it is apparent that over night they have experienced a change of feeling
                    as a resentful tone has crept into newspaper articles which express considerable
                    apprehension over the status of the immigration question. The <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Jiji</hi>, not accustomed to speaking rashly on
                    questions of foreign relations, says: “No nation retaining the <pb facs="0469"
                        n="375" xml:id="pg375"/> least trace of its self-respect could tolerate the
                    discrimination aimed at by the Johnson bill. The bill strikes at the very
                    foundation of American-Japanese friendship.” While the chauvinist <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Yorodzu</hi> says: “America is now taking advantage of
                    the weakness of the Japanese brought about by the earthquake and intends to
                    insult our nation.”</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Woods</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d282-f1" n="43" xml:id="d282-f1-content">
